<h1>Cleanroom Conveyors</h1>
<p class="usa-intro">Cleanroom conveyors operate within ISO 14644-1 classified environments, from ISO Class 5 (3,520 particles per cubic meter at 0.5 microns and larger, the former Class 100) through ISO Class 8 (3,520,000 particles per cubic meter), for pharmaceutical, medical device, and semiconductor manufacturing. These conveyors use electropolished stainless steel or anodized aluminum frames, non-shedding belt materials, sealed bearings, and enclosed drives to minimize particle generation that would compromise the cleanroom classification. A qualified supplier can document particle emission test results and materials of construction for the specific model.</p>
<figure class="photo"><img src="/img/photos/cleanroom-mk-guf-p-2000-smooth-motor.webp" width="1000" height="1000" alt="The drive end of a belt conveyor with a white belt on an aluminum frame, a gearmotor in a smooth unfinned housing mounted at the end pulley, and a stand on casters." loading="lazy" decoding="async" /><figcaption>The drive end of an mk GUF-P 2000 belt conveyor from the maker&#39;s cleanroom range. mk describes this version, fitted with a smooth-bodied motor, as designed to minimize wear and make cleaning easy. <span class="photo-credit">Photo: <a href="https://www.mk-group.com/en/products/conveyor-technology/special-conveyors/cleanroom-conveyor.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">mk Technology Group</a></span></figcaption></figure>
<h2 id="iso-heading">ISO Cleanroom Classifications</h2>
<p><a href="https://www.iso.org/standard/53394.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">ISO 14644-1</a> defines cleanroom classifications by the maximum allowable particle concentration per cubic meter at specified particle sizes. The classification determines how stringent the conveyor design must be, each step from ISO Class 8 down to ISO Class 5 tightens the requirements on materials, surface finishes, bearings, drives, and belt selection.</p>
<table>
<thead>
<tr>
<th>ISO Class</th>
<th>Max particles/m3 (≥0.5 µm)</th>
<th>FDA aseptic guidance use</th>
<th>Common conveyor practice</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr>
<td>ISO 5 (Class 100)</td>
<td>3,520</td>
<td>Critical area where sterile product and containers are exposed</td>
<td>Enclosed, HEPA-protected, qualified materials, drives kept out of the airflow path</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>ISO 6 (Class 1,000)</td>
<td>35,200</td>
<td>Optional, stricter class for the area next to the aseptic line</td>
<td>Enclosed drives, particle-tested belts</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>ISO 7 (Class 10,000)</td>
<td>352,000</td>
<td>Minimum for the area immediately next to the aseptic line</td>
<td>Sealed bearings, smooth finishes, stainless or anodized aluminum</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>ISO 8 (Class 100,000)</td>
<td>3,520,000</td>
<td>Less critical activities such as equipment cleaning, common isolator background</td>
<td>Stainless frame with sealed bearings</td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<p>Source note: particle limits and class uses from <a href="https://www.fda.gov/regulatory-information/search-fda-guidance-documents/sterile-drug-products-produced-aseptic-processing-current-good-manufacturing-practice" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">FDA, Sterile Drug Products Produced by Aseptic Processing (2004), Table 1 and Section IV</a>, which match ISO 14644-1. The conveyor practice column is common industry practice, not a requirement of either document.</p>
<p>The conveyor's contribution to room particle count depends on its surface area, operating speed, belt material, bearing type, and maintenance condition. The same conveyor can be insignificant in an ISO 8 room and a problem in an ISO 5 zone. <a href="https://www.iso.org/standard/91615.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">ISO 14644-14</a> gives a method for assessing whether a piece of equipment is suitable for a given ISO class by the airborne particle concentration it causes. Ask suppliers for results to that method at your operating speed.</p>
<h2 id="particle-heading">Particle Generation Sources</h2>
<p>Every moving part in a conveyor generates particles. The primary sources are belt-to-wear-strip friction, bearing seal abrasion, drive component wear, and belt splice or joint degradation. Cleanroom conveyor design addresses each source systematically.</p>
<h3>Belt-to-Wear-Strip Contact</h3>
<p>The belt slides over wear strips (slider bed) or rides on rollers along the conveyor bed. Belt-to-wear-strip friction is the largest particle source in a <a href="/conveyor-types/flat-belt-conveyors/">flat belt conveyor</a>. Standard UHMW wear strips shed visible particles under normal operation. Cleanroom conveyors use polished stainless steel slider beds or low-friction engineered polymer wear strips that generate fewer particles. Some designs replace slider beds with precision rollers to eliminate sliding friction entirely.</p>
<h3>Bearings</h3>
<p>Standard open or shielded bearings release grease mist and metal particles into the air. Cleanroom conveyors use permanently sealed bearings with food-grade or cleanroom-rated grease that does not migrate. Double-sealed (2RS) bearings with contact seals prevent particle escape. For ISO 5 and 6 environments, designers often move bearings and drives below the work surface or outside the critical zone. Through-wall layouts need care: <a href="https://health.ec.europa.eu/system/files/2022-08/20220825_gmp-an1_en_0.pdf" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">EU GMP Annex 1</a> (clause 5.8) says a conveyor belt should not pass through a partition between a Grade A or B area and a lower-grade area unless the belt itself is continually sterilised, for example in a sterilising tunnel.</p>
<h3>Drive Components</h3>
<p>Gear motors, chains, belts, and sprockets generate particles through friction and wear. Cleanroom conveyors enclose all drive components in sealed housings with gaskets. Internal drive cavities may use positive pressure with filtered air to prevent particle escape. Direct-drive motors (no gearbox, no belt, no chain) eliminate the particle sources associated with power transmission components. Servo direct drives provide the cleanest solution but cost more than geared motor drives.</p>
<h3>Belt Splices</h3>
<p>Belt splices (where belt ends are joined) create a discontinuity that generates particles as it passes over rollers and wear strips. Finger splices generate fewer particles than mechanical fastener splices. Welded or fused splices (thermal bonding) eliminate the overlap and produce the smoothest joint. For the highest cleanroom ratings, endless belts (no splice) eliminate this source entirely, the belt is manufactured to the exact conveyor length with no joint.</p>
<h2 id="materials-heading"><a href="/resources/conveyor-cost-guide/">Frame &amp; Belt Materials</a></h2>
<h3>Frame Materials</h3>
<p>Cleanroom conveyor frames use two primary materials: <a href="/conveyor-types/stainless-steel-conveyors/">stainless steel</a> (304 or 316L) and anodized aluminum. Stainless steel provides superior corrosion resistance, handles aggressive cleaning chemicals, and does not shed particles when properly finished. Electropolished stainless steel presents the smoothest surface for cleaning and minimal particle harborage.</p>
<p>Anodized aluminum offers a lighter-weight alternative at lower cost. Hard-coat (Type III) anodizing creates a durable, non-shedding surface that resists most cleanroom cleaning chemicals. Aluminum frames weigh much less than equivalent stainless frames, which matters when conveyors are mounted on adjustable-height stands or cantilevered from walls. Modular aluminum profile conveyor systems are common in ISO 7 and 8 cleanrooms because their extruded construction allows fast reconfiguration and clean-in-place compatibility.</p>
<figure class="photo"><img src="/img/photos/cleanroom-mk-guf-p-2000-hatch-discharge.webp" width="800" height="807" alt="A narrow belt conveyor with stainless side guides on an aluminum profile stand runs up to a white closure hatch in a white-walled room." loading="lazy" decoding="async" /><figcaption>An mk GUF-P 2000 belt conveyor on an aluminum profile stand, carrying product out through a fire protection hatch in the wall. mk describes this unit as optimized for technical cleanliness. <span class="photo-credit">Photo: <a href="https://www.mk-group.com/en/products/conveyor-technology/special-conveyors/cleanroom-conveyor.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">mk Technology Group</a></span></figcaption></figure>
<h3>Belt Materials</h3>
<p>Cleanroom belt materials must meet three criteria: low particle generation, no outgassing of volatile organic compounds (VOCs), and resistance to cleanroom cleaning agents. Standard PVC belts fail on all three, they shed plasticizer particles, outgas phthalates and chlorinated compounds, and degrade under IPA (isopropyl alcohol) and hydrogen peroxide cleaning.</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Polyurethane (silicone-free):</strong> The most common cleanroom belt material. Low particle generation, minimal outgassing, good chemical resistance. Silicone-free formulation prevents silicone contamination, critical in pharmaceutical and semiconductor environments where silicone causes adhesion and coating defects.</li>
<li><strong>PTFE-coated fiberglass:</strong> Extremely low friction, excellent chemical resistance, and minimal particle generation. High temperature resistance. Used in pharmaceutical drying and coating applications within cleanrooms.</li>
<li><strong>Monofilament polyester mesh:</strong> Open mesh belt for cleanroom ovens, dryers, and processes requiring airflow through the belt. Monofilament construction (single strand, not braided) minimizes particle trapping in the belt structure.</li>
<li><strong>Stainless steel flat wire:</strong> For high-temperature or ultra-clean applications. No organic material to outgas. Particle generation limited to metal-to-metal contact points.</li>
</ul>
<p>Request particle emission test data for the belt and conveyor at the expected operating speed before specifying a belt for ISO 5 or 6 environments. Ask which test method was used, such as the ISO 14644-14 equipment assessment, and under what load.</p>
<h2 id="finish-heading">Surface Finishes &amp; Outgassing</h2>
<p>Surface roughness (Ra value) determines how easily a surface can be cleaned and how many particles it harbors in microscopic valleys and scratches. Smoother surfaces harbor fewer particles and clean more effectively. No ISO 14644 class sets a surface roughness value. The buyer's user requirement specification (URS) sets the maximum Ra for product-contact and non-contact surfaces.</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Mechanically polished or brushed:</strong> common for non-contact frames in ISO 7-8 rooms.</li>
<li><strong>Electropolished after mechanical polishing:</strong> common for product-contact surfaces and ISO 5-6 equipment.</li>
<li><strong>Mirror electropolish:</strong> used for the most demanding applications, such as sterile injectables or wafer handling.</li>
</ul>
<p>For fluid-contact bioprocessing equipment, <a href="https://www.asme.org/codes-standards/find-codes-standards/bpe-bioprocessing-equipment-(1)" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">ASME BPE</a> defines surface finish designations with maximum Ra values for mechanically polished and electropolished stainless steel. Conveyors are outside its scope, but many pharmaceutical buyers reference its designations in a URS. State the Ra in µm or µin and how it will be measured.</p>
<p>Electropolishing removes a controlled layer of surface metal through an electrochemical process, smoothing peaks and valleys, and creating a chromium-rich passive layer that resists corrosion. It is common on pharmaceutical cleanroom conveyors. GMP itself does not name a finish: it requires that product-contact surfaces not be reactive, additive, or absorptive.</p>
<h3>Outgassing</h3>
<p>Outgassing is the release of volatile organic compounds (VOCs) from materials at room temperature or under process conditions. In semiconductor fabs, outgassed compounds deposit on wafer surfaces and cause defects. In pharmaceutical cleanrooms, VOCs can contaminate drug products. Facilities that care about outgassing specify a test method, either a facility-specific protocol or a published screening method. <a href="https://store.astm.org/e0595-15r21.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">ASTM E595</a> is a common screen, but it heats samples to 125 °C in a vacuum for 24 hours, so it does not reproduce room-temperature cleanroom conditions. Treat it as a comparative screen and confirm the method fits your process.</p>
<p>Common outgassing culprits in conveyor construction include PVC belt materials (plasticizer migration), rubber seals and gaskets (sulfur compounds), adhesive-backed labels and tape, and lubricants. Cleanroom conveyor design eliminates these materials or substitutes low-outgassing alternatives, silicone-free polyurethane belts, PTFE seals, laser-etched identification marks (no labels), and dry-running or sealed lubrication.</p>
<h2 id="gmp-heading">GMP Compliance &amp; Pharmaceutical Requirements</h2>
<p><a href="/industries/pharmaceutical-conveyors/">Pharmaceutical conveyors</a> in cleanrooms must comply with cGMP (current Good Manufacturing Practice) regulations enforced by the FDA (<a href="https://www.ecfr.gov/current/title-21/chapter-I/subchapter-C/part-211/subpart-D" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">21 CFR Part 211, Subpart D</a>) and equivalent international rules (EU GMP Annex 1 for sterile manufacturing). Under 21 CFR 211.63 to 211.67, equipment must be designed and located to allow cleaning and maintenance, product-contact surfaces must not be reactive, additive, or absorptive, and lubricants must not contact the product. Those rules affect conveyor design in several specific ways.</p>
<h3>Cleanability</h3>
<p>All conveyor surfaces must be accessible for cleaning and inspection. No hidden cavities, dead legs, or areas that cannot be reached with cleaning agents. Fasteners use smooth-head bolts (no hex socket heads that trap particles) or are eliminated in favor of welded construction. Frame joints are continuous-welded and ground smooth, no crevices for particle or microbial harborage.</p>
<p>Pharmaceutical cleanroom conveyors support two cleaning regimes: manual wipe-down with IPA or approved disinfectant (daily or per batch), and periodic deep cleaning with hydrogen peroxide vapor (HPV) or vaporized peracetic acid for room-level decontamination. Belt and frame materials must withstand repeated exposure to these agents without degradation, discoloration, or particle generation.</p>
<h3>Clean-in-Place (CIP)</h3>
<p>CIP systems clean conveyor surfaces automatically using spray nozzles that apply cleaning solution, rinse water, and sanitizer without disassembly. CIP is common in <a href="/industries/food-conveyors/sanitary/">sanitary food conveyors</a> and applies to pharmaceutical cleanroom conveyors in certain applications, particularly liquid and semi-solid pharmaceutical manufacturing where product contact occurs. CIP-capable conveyors use sealed frame construction, welded joints, and belt materials rated for CIP chemical exposure (caustic, acid, sanitizer cycles).</p>
<h3>Documentation and Traceability</h3>
<p>GMP requires documented evidence that the conveyor meets specifications. Conveyor manufacturers provide material certificates (mill certs for stainless steel), surface finish measurements, weld inspection records, and particle generation test reports. An IQ/OQ (Installation Qualification / Operational Qualification) protocol verifies that the installed conveyor meets design specifications and operates within cleanroom requirements. Equipment suppliers that serve pharmaceutical customers provide IQ/OQ documentation packages as part of the conveyor delivery.</p>
<h2 id="hepa-heading">HEPA-Compatible Conveyor Design</h2>
<p>Cleanrooms maintain particle control through unidirectional (laminar) airflow from ceiling-mounted HEPA filters downward through the work zone and out through floor-level returns. Conveyors must not obstruct this airflow pattern. A solid conveyor frame placed under a HEPA filter bank creates a turbulent wake zone where particles accumulate instead of being swept away.</p>
<p>HEPA-compatible conveyor design principles include minimizing frame cross-section in the airflow path, using open frame construction (tube profiles instead of solid panels), positioning drives and motors below the work zone or outside the classified area, and orienting the conveyor to align with, not perpendicular to, the dominant airflow direction.</p>
<h3>Enclosed Conveyor Systems</h3>
<p>For ISO 5 environments, the conveyor and product travel inside a sealed enclosure with its own HEPA-filtered air supply. The enclosure maintains positive pressure relative to the surrounding room, preventing unfiltered air from entering. Transfer points between the enclosure and processing equipment use sealed pass-throughs or rapid-close doors that minimize air exchange.</p>
<p>Enclosed conveyor tunnels with integrated HEPA filtration create a localized ISO 5 environment within a less-classified room (ISO 7 or 8). This approach costs less than classifying the entire room to ISO 5. The conveyor enclosure becomes part of the facility's cleanroom qualification, its filter integrity, air change rate, and particle count are tested during room certification.</p>
<h3>Isolator and RABS Integration</h3>
<p>In sterile pharmaceutical manufacturing, conveyors pass through isolators (sealed enclosures with glove ports) and RABS (Restricted Access Barrier Systems). The conveyor penetrates the isolator wall through a sealed port with inflatable gaskets or labyrinth seals. The conveyor section inside the isolator must be sterilized, typically with vaporized hydrogen peroxide (VHP), along with the rest of the isolator interior before production.</p>
<p>Conveyor belt material must withstand repeated VHP sterilization cycles without degradation. Get the belt supplier's VHP exposure data for your cycle. Silicone-free polyurethane and PTFE belts are commonly used. Stainless steel belt surfaces provide the highest chemical resistance for isolator applications. Keep drive components outside the isolator where possible, and check any belt that crosses a Grade A or B boundary against Annex 1 clause 5.8: unless the belt is continually sterilised, use a separate conveyor on each side with a transfer point instead.</p>
<h2 id="apps-heading">Cleanroom Conveyor Applications</h2>
<h3>Pharmaceutical Fill-Finish</h3>
<p>Sterile injectable drugs (vials, syringes, ampoules) are filled in ISO 5 environments. Conveyors transport empty containers from the washer/depyrogenation tunnel into the filling isolator, through the filling and stoppering stations, and out to capping and inspection. Line speed is set by the filler. The conveyor must not disturb unidirectional airflow or add particles to the critical zone around open containers, which FDA guidance holds to ISO 5.</p>
<h3>Medical Device Assembly</h3>
<p>Medical devices, implants, surgical instruments, diagnostic cartridges, are commonly assembled in ISO 7 or 8 cleanrooms. Conveyors transport components through assembly stations, adhesive curing zones, inspection cameras, and packaging. Pallet-transfer conveyors with servo indexing position components precisely for automated assembly. Conveyor surfaces must be biocompatible or at least non-contaminating, no material transfer from belt to product.</p>
<h3>Semiconductor Manufacturing</h3>
<p>Semiconductor fabs run critical process areas at ISO 5 or cleaner. Wafer transport within process tools is highly specialized (robotic handling in sealed mini-environments). Between process steps, wafer cassettes (FOUPs, Front Opening Unified Pods) travel on overhead transport systems (OHT) or automated material handling systems (AMHS). Conveyors in less stringent semiconductor support areas transport materials, components, and packaging with cleanroom-compatible construction.</p>
<h3>Optics and Photonics</h3>
<p>Optical component manufacturing (lenses, mirrors, fiber optics) requires cleanroom environments to prevent particle contamination on optical surfaces. Conveyors transport optical elements through coating, inspection, and assembly. Vibration control is critical, conveyor drives must produce minimal vibration that could misalign optical components during bonding or measurement. Direct-drive servo motors with vibration-damped frames address this requirement.</p>
<h2 id="spec-heading">Specifying a Cleanroom Conveyor</h2>
<p>Cleanroom conveyor specifications start with the ISO classification of the target environment. The classification drives every material and design decision. A conveyor for an ISO 8 room requires sealed bearings and smooth stainless steel or anodized aluminum frames, standard cleanroom practice. A conveyor for an ISO 5 isolator requires enclosed drives, particle-tested endless belts, electropolished surfaces, and VHP sterilization compatibility, a fundamentally different and more expensive design.</p>
<p>Key specification parameters for cleanroom conveyors:</p>
<ol>
<li><strong>ISO classification</strong> of the operating environment (determines material and design requirements)</li>
<li><strong>Product type and weight</strong>, pharmaceutical vials, medical device components, semiconductor wafers</li>
<li><strong>Line speed and indexing requirements</strong>, continuous motion or stop-and-go</li>
<li><strong>Cleaning protocol</strong>, wipe-down (IPA), CIP, VHP sterilization</li>
<li><strong>Frame material</strong>, 304SS, 316L SS (electropolished), anodized aluminum</li>
<li><strong>Belt material</strong>, silicone-free PU, PTFE, polyester mesh, stainless steel</li>
<li><strong>Surface finish</strong>, Ra value for product-contact and non-contact surfaces</li>
<li><strong>Drive location</strong>, inside classified area (enclosed) or outside (through-wall)</li>
<li><strong>Documentation</strong>, material certs, surface finish reports, IQ/OQ protocols</li>
</ol>
<p>For budgeting, the main cost drivers are the ISO class, frame material and finish, drive enclosure or through-wall placement, belt qualification, sterilization compatibility, and the qualification documentation package. An ISO 7-8 conveyor in standard stainless or anodized aluminum costs far less than an ISO 5 design with enclosed drives, electropolished surfaces, and particle-tested belts. For a broader overview of <a href="/industries/pharmaceutical-conveyors/">pharmaceutical conveyor systems</a> beyond cleanroom-specific requirements, see our parent guide.</p>
