<h2 id="faq-heading">Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
<div class="usa-accordion usa-accordion--bordered usa-accordion--multiselectable" data-allow-multiple="">
<h3 class="usa-accordion__heading"><button type="button" class="usa-accordion__button" aria-expanded="true" aria-controls="faq-1">What is a cleanroom conveyor?</button></h3>
<div id="faq-1" class="usa-accordion__content usa-prose">
<p>A conveyor designed to operate within ISO 14644-1 classified environments without generating particles that compromise the cleanroom rating. Uses smooth, non-shedding surfaces (electropolished SS, anodized aluminum), sealed bearings, enclosed drives, and non-outgassing materials. Serves <a href="/industries/pharmaceutical-conveyors/">pharmaceutical</a>, medical device, and semiconductor manufacturing.</p>

</div>
<h3 class="usa-accordion__heading"><button type="button" class="usa-accordion__button" aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="faq-2">What ISO classes require specialized conveyors?</button></h3>
<div id="faq-2" class="usa-accordion__content usa-prose">
<p>No standard assigns conveyor features to ISO classes, but common practice is: ISO 7-8 use standard stainless steel with sealed bearings. ISO 6 calls for enclosed drives and particle-tested belts. ISO 5 calls for fully enclosed systems, HEPA-filtered enclosures, and qualified materials. EU GMP Annex 1 says a belt should not pass through a partition between a Grade A or B area and a lower-grade area unless it is continually sterilised.</p>

</div>
<h3 class="usa-accordion__heading"><button type="button" class="usa-accordion__button" aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="faq-3">What belt materials are cleanroom compatible?</button></h3>
<div id="faq-3" class="usa-accordion__content usa-prose">
<p>Silicone-free polyurethane (most common), PTFE-coated fiberglass, monofilament polyester mesh, and stainless steel flat wire. These generate minimal particles, do not outgas VOCs, and resist IPA and hydrogen peroxide cleaning. Standard PVC and rubber belts are not cleanroom-compatible.</p>

</div>
<h3 class="usa-accordion__heading"><button type="button" class="usa-accordion__button" aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="faq-4">How do you validate a cleanroom conveyor?</button></h3>
<div id="faq-4" class="usa-accordion__content usa-prose">
<p>Validation includes particle generation testing (measuring airborne particles at the belt surface during operation), outgassing testing (VOC emissions), surface finish verification (Ra values), and operational qualification under production conditions. The conveyor must not cause the room to exceed its ISO classification during operation.</p>

</div>
</div>
<h2 id="specify-cleanroom-grade-conveyor-systems">Specify Cleanroom-Grade Conveyor Systems</h2>
<p>From ISO 8 packaging lines to ISO 5 sterile fill-finish, cleanroom conveyor design determines whether your classified environment maintains its rating under production conditions.</p>
